Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
black women in this country have fought  for equal pay and equal access to loans  for generations  so they can start their own businesses  and buy homes but new data out today  from goldman sachs shows they are still  two times more likely to live in poverty  and their average hourly wages are 34  less than white men they're also less  likely to own their own companies  we met one business owner her name is  jessica johnson cope who is trying to  change that  her company which has been in her family  for generations provides professional  security to businesses across the new  york metropolitan area  here's her story in her own words  my name is jessica johnson cope i am the  president  and ceo of johnson security bureau the  oldest black owned security firm  in new york state  johnson security bureau is a third  generation family owned and operated  security services firm based in the  bronx new york  founded by my grandparents dorothy and  wilbert johnson they started the  business in their house in 1962  to create a company where they could  provide jobs for people in the community  you may not see a lot of women-owned  firms in our neighborhood  you may not see a lot of big businesses  coming here  but when they see johnson security  bureau it gives them a sense of  hope that there is someone who looks  like them  who's achieved some level of success  the goldman sachs 10 000 small  businesses program was launched in 2009  as an answer to the financial crash of  2008  and i got the chance to interview with  one of the members of the executive team  at goldman sachs  she said what if you had resources what  if someone showed you  how you could better run your business  what if you could use  other people's money and in that way put  more people to work  and have a greater impact on your  community and we've had sensational  results  i mean we've gone from 16 employees to  over 160  our revenues have increased  significantly if you  have the desire and you have the right  tools  you can find success whatever that means  to you  16 employees to 160 that's impressive  growth goldman sachs  is announcing a new 10 billion dollar  plan to invest in jobs  health care and housing for black women  chairman and ceo david solomon joins us  with the head of the firm's  urban investment group margaret anadu  this is their first tv interview about  the announcement good morning to you  both and i'm very glad it's warm enough  in new york that you're both outside on  the roof great to see you good  well we're we're really happy to be with  you this morning and it's  it is definitely almost warm enough to  be out on the roof  i'm probably just a little bit  underdressed all right david let's  let's get you going and warm you up  first how will goldman sachs invest that  10  million billion dollars to help black  women  well we're very very excited about this  program and we've been working for quite  some time  to think about a way that we can have a  lasting economic impact  the earnings gaps and also the wealth  gaps that exist for black women  who are so important in the community  based on all they're doing are very very  significant and so we've really been  working  toward putting together a longer term  plan to disperse this capital in ways  that can really drive  broader economic participation narrow  gaps  and really help drive more participation  in the economy i'd like margaret to talk  a little bit about some of the things  that we're thinking specifically about  to drive those results  over time go ahead margaret yeah  so as we think about the 10 billion  dollars and and the impact that we can  have here you know you heard from  from jessica johnson just what it means  to have that access to capital  but we want to do something much broader  here and that's why we want to invest in  health care  education housing child care because  stepping back right these are the  foundational elements in a black woman's  life that really drive economic  prosperity  and we think when we can start to narrow  some of those opportunity gaps  we unlock her potential we lift her up  and that's not just important for those  black women  but also we're going to see the impact  on their families on our communities  and we feel pretty strongly we're going  to drive progress overall margaret in  2018  you were the youngest black woman to be  named a partner at goldman sachs  what needs to be done to get more black  women in leadership positions  you know i think even an initiative like  this and what we're building right this  is about the  infrastructure that allows more black  women to be at the table  and so when we can get more black women  that college degree when we can help  with the upskilling when we can help  black women  really advance in their careers you're  going to see more you know black women  at the table and quite frankly i think  to sort of drive the type of progress  in initiatives like this that we need to  see well seeing you standing there side  by side with david solomon i think sends  a very important message margaret i want  to go back  to you david because i love the the the  question that jessica  posed in the piece what would you do if  you had the resources that  she was asked that by goldman sachs so  why is this important to you i want you  to take us behind the scenes  about your thinking on this and it's  more than just making opportunities for  black women in business  absolutely gail it's much more than that  and i think the the firm has learned a  lot over the last five years  as we have over the last 15 years as  we've made investments  in other programs i know that you're  familiar with like our ten thousand  women program  and our ten thousand small business  program that jessica is highlighting  what we've learned is that if we take  our research  and we really dig deep to try to  understand where there are problems or  gaps in our society  and then we take our resources our  capital our network  our ability to give people advice and  help them gain access to capital  we can create a very positive impact  over time we can help lift people up we  can help them participate and by the way  it's great for those people  but more importantly for our society it  helps us all economically  if we can close the wage gap for black  women in this country we can create  1.7 million more jobs and we can  increase  economic gdp output by about 450 billion  dollars  so we all benefit from broader  participation and that's why  the research that we're putting out this  morning black womenomics  helps us understand where we can all  make investments to help drive our  society forward  david the house is expected to uh to  pass today to vote today on the 1.9  trillion dollar relief package the covet  relief package  what else needs to be done i mean do you  think it's enough to begin with and what  else do you think needs to be done to  get the economy going again  well i think the most important thing i  think the economy is starting to do  better as we've all seen  as we are making progress on the  pandemic the most important gaining item  for us to get more economic recovery is  to continue to advance on the vaccine  and for us to get to a place where  people feel healthy  safe and secure that they can operate  out in the world in a way that's normal  and they can participate so the biggest  thing right now is to continue to get  vaccines distributed continue to get the  uptake  so that we can move forward and get past  this horrible health crisis  which has really just amplified some of  these inequalities  that we see in our society david  sullivan solomon  margaret anadu thank you both for being  with us this morning  we appreciate it

